  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nBroad high pressure will become centered overhead today through\nSaturday. This will continue fair weather with highs in the 80s\nand lows in the 50s. Conditions will be favorable for sea\nbreezes that will push inland during the afternoons. High\npressure will continue fair weather Sunday as the center slowly\ndrifts east of the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper-level low moving through Hudson Bay will amplify a trough \nover the Northeast U.S. by early in the week. The low-level response \nwill be a surface low that likely tracks across New England or \nsouthern Quebec. With it still being ~5 days out, plenty of model \nspread exists. Next rain chance centered only general the GFS and \nGEFS ensemble are further south, less amplified, and more \nprogressive, while the ECMWF/EPS ensemble are both further north/more \namplified with a surface low closing off up through 500 mb. Dry \nweather should make a return towards the end of the week, but the \nexact timing of that remains fuzzy.\n\nIn terms of local impacts, generally a similar but less anomalous \nsetup to the downpours/t-storms that affected us these last few \ndays. Pretty confident we will see PWATs reach at least 1.5\" with \ndew points well into the 60s, which should support heavy downpours. \nOnce again see a signal for a bit of a cold pool aloft with lowering \nheights and temperatures aloft, which would support modest \ninstability developing especially with any sunny breaks. As such \nwill likely have a few waves of downpours/t-storms both Tuesday and \nWednesday, with the greatest concentration during the afternoon \nhours each day when instability is maximized. Despite the increasing \nhumidity, should have seasonable if not slightly cool temperatures, \ngiven the increased cloud cover and lowering 500 mb heights/1000-500 \nmb thickness by midweek.",
